          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Gustave Flaubert</strong><br>\nGustave Flaubert was born on December 12, 1821, in Rouen, France, to a family of doctors. As a child, he was exposed to literature and art, which sparked his interest in writing. Despite his father's hopes for him to become a doctor, Flaubert was drawn to the world of storytelling and writing. His education at the Collège Royal de Rouen laid the groundwork for his literary ambitions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Flaubert's Writing Style</strong><br>\nFlaubert was known for his precise writing style, seeking the \"mot juste\" or the most appropriate word. He believed in the principle of finding the perfect phrase for every situation, which often meant he spent weeks on a single page. His commitment to style and form made him a major influence on the literary world and an advocate for literary realism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Madame Bovary</strong><br>\nMadame Bovary is Flaubert's most famous novel, published in 1857. It tells the story of Emma Bovary, a doctor's wife who seeks escape from her mundane life through a series of extramarital affairs and lavish spending, leading to her downfall. The novel was controversial at the time, resulting in a trial for obscenity, but it has since become a classic for its exploration of human desires and societal pressures.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
