          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Flaubert's Influence on Realism</strong><br>\nFlaubert is often celebrated for his contributions to literary realism. He challenged the romanticism of his time, focusing instead on the ordinary aspects of life and the complexities of human character. By presenting life as it was, with a focus on the details that make up everyday existence, he helped shape the realist movement in literature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legal Troubles with Art</strong><br>\nThe publication of \"Madame Bovary\" brought legal troubles to Flaubert. The French government charged him with obscenity for the novel's frank portrayal of adultery. However, he was acquitted, and the trial brought attention to the novel, helping to solidify its place in literary history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Flaubert's Travels</strong><br>\nFlaubert traveled extensively after the success of \"Madame Bovary.\" His journeys took him to the Middle East, North Africa, and various parts of Europe. The experiences he gathered from his travels influenced his later writings, including the historical novel \"Salammbo\" and the unfinished work \"Bouvard et Pecuchet.\"</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
