          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relationship with Louise Colet</strong><br>\nLouise Colet was Flaubert's lover and confidant for many years. She was a poet and writer who engaged with Flaubert in a passionate and tumultuous relationship. Their correspondence has provided significant insight into Flaubert's life and thought processes, as well as into the literary circles of the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Flaubert's Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nFlaubert died on May 8, 1880, leaving behind a legacy as one of France's greatest novelists. His literary works continue to be studied and admired for their style, depth, and precision. He influenced many writers who followed him, including Marcel Proust and the naturalists Émile Zola and Guy de Maupassant.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Posthumous Publications and Recognition</strong><br>\nAfter his death, Flaubert's reputation continued to grow. Unfinished works, including \"Bouvard et Pecuchet,\" were published posthumously, and his place in the Western literary canon was cemented. His correspondence with contemporaries like George Sand and Émile Zola also became valuable literature, offering insights into the intellectual debates of his era.</p>\n"
              },
